### Page 3 of 9

```text
attorney for the agent, explained the ordinance establishing the district, DEQ involvement and
the final plat were all affiliated and that the Rucao district needs to be established prior to
finalizing other areas.  Carl Anderson, manager, DEQ Voluntary Remediation Program for
contaminated sites, advised the former landfill can be used for other purposes and that DEQ is
not opposed to certain redevelopment activities as long as it is done in a protective manner.  He
indicated that before finalizing and entering into a remedy agreement, the Rucao district must be
in place.  Discussion followed on proposed use of the area as soccer fields and possibly for
storage and office space; City staff’s request to DEQ to receive a letter confirming allowable
property uses prior to establishment of the district, and letter (unsigned) staff had received from
DEQ stating future intended usage was only for soccer fields.  Mandy Bernard, Development
office, advised the DEQ letter received had been distributed to governing body members, that
conditional issues are still pending, and that staff is requesting postponement on the ordinance to
receive further information that had been requested.  Mr. Basom stated uses can not be
controlled and restricted until the restrictive overlay district is in place. Mr. Anderson provided
information on the property restricted use control agreement process between the parties
involved and the voluntary remediation program, and stated unrestricted use would not be
allowed, however, recreation and certain commercial/industrial uses may be allowed.  Mr.
Basom advised a previous City ordinance establishing another Rucao district had been used as a
template for this proposed action.  Mr. Collins stated there was no recommendation on the item
from Public Services Committee.  Mr. Collins moved to approve on second reading with staff
recommendations #1 through 4, seconded by Mr. Pierson. It was noted that Development staff is
seeking a letter from DEQ that will outline appropriate land use activities for the site.  Ms.
Bernard stated Development has also requested information from the developer/agent pertaining
to maps depicting the former landfill and buildable areas, greenway connection, and land-use
and traffic considerations, and explained staff is requesting time to receive and review the
information.  Mr. Collins moved to postpone to January 26, 2009.  Motion died due to the lack of
a second.  It was noted the ordinance was being presented on second reading and, if approved
and further time to receive information was needed, consideration of a postponement could be
entertained on third reading.  Ms. Case raised a point of order involving parliamentary procedure
and stating of the question before the assembly with Mayor Spiker responding that he would
follow the previous ruling by Acting City Attorney Nicholas concerning governing body
discretion, and customary procedure, and called for a vote on the motion to approve the
ordinance on second reading.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing
body present with the exception of Mr. Laybourn voting “no”.
“AN ORDINANCE ANNEXING TO THE CITY OF CHEYENNE, WYOMING,
SWAN RANCH SITUATED IN SECTIONS 11 AND 12, T. 13N., R. 67W., OF THE 6
 P.M.,
TH
LARAMIE COUNTY, WYOMING (LOCATED ALONG CLEAR CREEK SOUTH OF AND
INCLUDING I-80 RIGHT-OF-WAY AND EAST OF AND INCLUDING I-25 RIGHT-OF-
WAY).”  Gay Woodhouse, attorney representing several property owners in the area, spoke in
opposition to the ordinance, commenting on the following topics: Estimated infrastructure costs
involving potential future annexation to area properties; access points; assurance to residents in
the area concerning traffic concerns; suggestion of consideration of a conservation easement or
dedication; physical boundary of the area and state statute annexation criteria; potential future
development types; health, safety and welfare concerns, and area property owners’ investments
in their land.  Also expressing concerns with the proposed annexation were  Brenda Rath, John
Fafoutakis and Leo Garcia.  Comments included the following topics: Potential future
annexation infrastructure costs to adjacent property owners (informational handout submitted by
```

