```text
Ms. Rath on file with office of City Clerk); 201 Agreement and sewer and water services; real
estate valuations in the area, and developer vs. area property owners’ interests.  Ms. Case raised
a point of order questioning discussion from the audience being held without a motion on the
floor with Mayor Spiker ruling that City ordinance provides for input from the public prior to a
motion on the floor.  Mr. Collins moved to approve on second reading, seconded by Mr.
Segrave. Upon inquiry, Brad Emmons, AVI Engineering, agent for the applicant, provided
information on the property and development planning time frame involving annexation, and
advised at this time the property owner has not disclosed future development plans.  Ms. Case
raised a point of parliamentary clarification concerning discussion while no motion is pending
with Mayor Spiker deferring to Acting City Attorney Nicholas for interpretation and response.
Attorney Nicholas advised the practice adopted by the governing body, and by City code, with
regard to how an issue is brought to the floor and presented for discussion before the governing
body is not inconsistent with Robert’s Rules of Order.  Upon inquiry, Brandon Cammarata,
Urban Planning office, provided information on developer infrastructure costs and
reimbursement
procedures
affecting
adjacent
property
owners
upon
future
annexation/development actions pursuant to City ordinance, and effects of provisions contained
in the 201 Agreement.  Ms. Woodhouse commented on potential future infrastructure
reimbursement costs to area property owners and affiliated notice of intent to collect
reimbursement forms filed with the County as related to title company lien searches involving
property ownership changes.  Discussion followed on property development in the area,
including the new proposed Speer interchange; unknown development plans for the property;
vision of area development as presented by consultants involved in the Urban Planning office’s
Uniform Development Code project, and water and sewer services.  Upon inquiry, Ms.
Woodhouse confirmed a meeting with the developer’s agent, Mr. Emmons, had been held but a
meeting directly with the developer had not been requested.  Discussion continued and included
the following topics: Communication between property owners, the developer and City Urban
Planning staff; possible legal recourse in opposition to the annexation by neighboring property
owners; annexations of county properties involving wells and septic systems; traffic impacts;
future financial concerns of nearby property owners; health, safety and welfare and protection of
water supplies; emergency response services; city growth; county pockets, and voluntary
annexation requests by property owners.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” - all members of the
governing body present with the exception of Ms. Case, Mr. Laybourn and Mr. Valdez voting
“no.”
