```text
opinion concerning the point of order that had been raised relative to past common and
customary practices involving the mayor’s recognition of speakers wishing to have the floor.
Attorney Nicholas opined that the maker of the motion had the opportunity to speak to the
motion when completion of the reading/making of the motion was done and the maker chose not
to do so; recognition to have the floor was then passed to Mr. Collins.  Mayor Spiker ruled he
would accept the opinion expressed by Mr. Nicholas and returned the floor to Mr. Collins.
Comment followed on the unknown costs to conduct the study and, if a request were received to
close the street, traffic study costs would be the financial responsibility of the applicant. Mr.
Collins moved to call for the question (to limit debate), seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Upon
comment involving discussion on the motion, Mayor Spiker advised the motion was not
debatable and subsequently ruled Mr. Laybourn out of order.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body present with the exception of Ms. Case and Mr. Laybourn
voting “no”.  Main motion failed.  Voting “yes”– Ms. Case and Mr. Laybourn.  Voting “no” –
Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Mr. Collins, Mr. Pierson, Mr. Segrave, Mr. Valdez and Mayor Spiker.
