ANNOUNCEMENTS/REPORTS/MOTIONS:
Announcement was made that the Sine Die meeting of the Governing Body will be held
at 6:00 p.m., and the First Regular Meeting of the Governing Body will be held at 6:30 p.m., on
Monday, January 5, 2009 in the Civic Center, 510 W. 20th Street; a reception to follow in the
Civic Center Lobby.
Announcement was made that upcoming City Council Committee meetings are
rescheduled as follows: Finance Committee at 11:00 a.m. in Room 122; Vouchers at 12:00 p.m.
in City Council Chambers; and Public Services Committee at 12:15 p.m. in Room 122, on
Tuesday, January 6, 2009.
Announcement was made of a public hearing to be held January 12, 2009 at 6:00 p.m., in
City Council Chambers, 2101 O’Neil Avenue, to discuss the sale of City-owned real property
located at 700 West Fox Farm Road, Cheyenne, Wyoming to Attention Homes, Inc. which sale
will benefit the economic development of the City of Cheyenne, pursuant to Wyo. Stat. § 15-1-
112(b)(i)(D).  The estimated appraised value of said property to be sold is $184,000 and will be
sold to Attention Homes, Inc. for $2,000.  This land will provide additional space to facilitate the
expansion and redevelopment of Attention Homes, Inc.
OTHER CITY BUSINESS:
John Fafoutakis commented on aircraft in the area he had recently noticed and Homeland
Security notification concerns.
Jan Nelson Schroll read a City Resolution, adopted in 2003, into the record which re-
committed Cheyenne to it’s historic preservation and requested City Council members work
with citizens to encourage preservation of historic properties through incentives and other
means; made comments in support of historic preservation, and expressed appreciation to Mr.
Laybourn for his past four years of service to his Council Ward.
Jeanie Tucker commented on moving of houses from the historic Rainsford District and
loss of history within the district.
Foy Jolly, spoke in support of the Henderson Basin Flood Control Project and
community drainage projects generally, and expressed appreciation to Mayor Spiker and
outgoing City Council members for their hard work as members of the governing body.
Due to a prior commitment, Mayor Spiker relinquished duties of the chair to Council
President Pierson and vacated Council Chambers for the remainder of the meeting.
Mr. Laybourn made statements concerning governing body meeting procedures,
including other City business, and commented on the Council’s Code of Conduct, and discussion
and participation restraints during his term in office.
Ms. Case commented on the purpose of Robert’s Rule of Order with regard to
parliamentary rights.
